      "id": "word-chicos--group-address",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "chicos",
      "answerEn": "guys; everyone (informal address to a group)",
      "noteEn": "Chicos literally refers to boys or young men, but speakers can use it informally to get the attention of a male or mixed group, much like ‘guys’ in English.",
      "example": { "es": "Chicos, venid aquí.", "en": "Guys, come here." },
      "tags": ["word", "address", "informal", "plural", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-habéis--you-all-have",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "habéis",
      "answerEn": "you all have (informal plural, mainly in Spain)",
      "noteEn": "Habéis is the present helper form of haber used with vosotros or vosotras. It comes before a completed-action form such as llegado or oído.",
      "example": { "es": "Habéis llegado temprano.", "en": "You all have arrived early." },
      "tags": ["word", "haber", "vosotros", "present perfect", "Spain", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-oído--heard",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "oído",
      "answerEn": "heard",
      "noteEn": "Oído is the completed-action form of oír, ‘to hear.’ It keeps the written accent on í and stays unchanged after a form of haber.",
      "example": { "es": "Nunca he oído esa canción.", "en": "I have never heard that song." },
      "tags": ["word", "oír", "past participle", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-dejad--stop-doing",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "dejad",
      "answerEn": "stop (doing); command to several people informally, mainly in Spain",
      "noteEn": "Dejad is the positive command form of dejar for vosotros or vosotras. When it is followed by de and another action, it tells the group to stop that activity.",
      "example": { "es": "Dejad de gritar.", "en": "Stop shouting." },
      "tags": ["word", "dejar", "command", "vosotros", "Spain", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-de--fixed-dejar-de",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "de",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; links dejar to the activity being stopped",
      "noteEn": "De is required between a form of dejar and the following action in the common pattern dejar de hacer algo, ‘to stop doing something.’",
      "example": { "es": "Dejamos de hablar.", "en": "We stopped talking." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed construction", "stopping", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-divertiros",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "divertiros",
      "answerEn": "having fun; enjoying yourselves (addressing several people informally)",
      "noteEn": "Divertiros is the form of divertirse, ‘to have fun,’ used for an informally addressed group in Spain: the attached se changes to os. After dejar de, English naturally uses ‘having fun’ for this activity.",
      "example": { "es": "Podéis divertiros aquí.", "en": "You all can have fun here." },
      "tags": ["word", "divertirse", "infinitive", "vosotros", "Spain", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-hacer-target-description",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "causa + hacer + persona o cosa + descripción",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that one thing makes someone or something have a certain quality?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of hacer with the affected person or thing and a word that describes the resulting quality.",
      "noteEn": "Hacer changes for the cause and time. A short affected-person word such as me, te, nos, or os stands before hacer: nos hace felices means ‘it makes us happy.’ The describing word matches the affected person or thing in singular or plural and, when its ending allows it, masculine or feminine.",
      "example": { "es": "La música nos hace felices.", "en": "Music makes us happy." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "hacer", "causation", "description", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-rock-band-rhythm-game",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "Rock Band · videojuego musical",
      "answerEn": "Rock Band is a rhythm video game played with instrument-shaped controllers while players perform along with recorded songs.",
      "noteEn": "Its plastic guitar has a strum bar, and the joke contrasts enjoying the game with being a ‘real’ rock band. The title text refers to the tactile click made by earlier guitar-game controllers.",
      "tags": ["concept", "video games", "music", "Rock Band"]
    }
  ],
